{"kind":"artifact","text":"task/docs-era repaired at exact head c0c9d4e110c3ba1ce065fcc06c823957746e44f9, addressing both findings in 3eb3e684dc4e37cd5b9c9fa5ebe70b5e5e2fdcd6. Both were overstatements in my repair rather than in the original text, and I reproduced both by execution before fixing them. F1: I claimed that everywhere else in the citation table, satisfying the rule gives an act its force. It does not — the fold also checks who signed. A promise citing a perfectly effective request addressed to alice, but authored by bob, comes back ineffective with the reason \"promise actor is not the requested performer\"; a report needs the promisor's own signature the same way, and ratify needs authority over that particular target. The table states what must resolve, not who may say it, so a citation earns an act a hearing rather than its force. The grant paragraph now rests on the distinction that actually holds: every other act settles its effectiveness once, when appended, while a grant can satisfy every rule, be effective, be ratified, and still confer nothing then or later. F2: I claimed revoking an explicit grant retires that role and nothing else. Measured, revoking operator takes a principal from [operator, participant, ratifier] to [participant], because ratifier was riding on operator with no grant of its own. I got that wrong by measuring only the ratifier case, where nothing is derived — so \"nothing else\" was true of the single instance that cannot distinguish the two hypotheses, which is the same error as a test passing for a reason other than the one it was written for. The text now states the rule and keeps both measurements, including the narrow-looking one, so a reader can see why it looks narrow. Gates green: vet, test, git diff --check, worktree clean.\n\nOne thing your report did not cover, and I would rather ask than assume: request 2ffd45bfd86e9762e6770568f3966c290dec37f5 named two heads, docs-era and task/status-projection at 16dc907bc7f801c459027e9b0d47928364439384. This report addresses only docs-era. Silence is not approval, so I am treating status-projection as still unreviewed.","body":{"commit":"c0c9d4e110c3ba1ce065fcc06c823957746e44f9","path":"docs/reference.md"}}